{"parentUuid":"21af8425-73ab-405d-abc1-f3ceec8d69e3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01BteZDvEuxGnTT5mBURTVwk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Direct answer to your slash-command question\n\n**No — `entire review --agent codex` does not actually invoke codex's `/review`.** Here is exactly what happens:\n\n1. Your settings list `/review` under `review.codex.skills` (it's the only built-in the codex picker offers, since `DiscoverReviewSkills` is stubbed).\n2. Before spawning codex, `expandCodexBuiltinReview` walks the skills list and **rewrites the literal string `/review` into a 28-word plain-text instruction**: `\"Review the current branch changes and report actionable findings. Prioritize correctness, regressions, security, and missing test coverage. Do not make code changes.\"`\n3. That paraphrased string (plus your always-prompt, per-run prompt, scope clause, checkpoint context) is what gets piped via stdin to `codex exec --json --skip-git-repo-check -`.\n4. Codex receives normal prompt text — no slash command, no special hint that this is \"a review.\" It improvises a review workflow from a short generic instruction.\n\nThe reason for the rewrite (per the code comment on `buildCodexReviewCmd`) is that codex's native `codex exec review --base <branch>` subcommand rejects an additional prompt — so we can't ask codex to do its native review *and* layer entire's scope clause / per-run instructions / checkpoint context on top.\n\n**So the \"fast standalone codex\" baseline you're comparing against is not the same workflow as `entire review --agent codex`.** When you open the codex TUI and type `/review`, codex runs its native review feature — whatever curated prompt, tool budget, and possibly model selection that includes. When `entire review --agent codex` runs, codex runs a *generic exec* with a 28-word paraphrase of what to do. That's a meaningful workflow asymmetry on its own, independent