{"parentUuid":"607738bc-9661-465b-9215-2637db36cb6f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01TEiAwQ8GvXQaHEwvarrM1s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ALHbMLaPrjXk67mn9BJPgf","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/review-fix-2/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-05-13-codex-review-slow-diagnostic-design.md","content":"# Why is `entire review --agent codex` 2-5x slower than `--agent claude-code`?\n\n**Status:** investigation / design\n**Date:** 2026-05-13\n**Author:** Peyton Montei (brainstormed with Claude)\n**Branch:** `slow-codex-in-review`\n\n## Motivation\n\n`entire review` runs review skills inside a spawned agent process. When running\nthe same review against the same branch, the codex spawn takes 2-5x longer than\nthe claude-code spawn on wall-clock — even though the user typically has *more*\nskills configured for claude than for codex. The user's anchor observation is\nthat the same `/review` invocation inside an interactive `codex` TUI session\nfinishes substantially faster than `entire review --agent codex`. This spec\ncharacterizes the cause and proposes fix shapes.\n\n## Phenomenon\n\n- Comparison: `entire review --agent claude-code` (multiple skills) vs.\n  `entire review --agent codex` (typically one skill, `/review`) on the same\n  branch.\n- Observation: claude finishes 2-5x faster than codex. Standalone interactive\n  `codex` running `/review` directly is also fast.\n- The streaming gap that PR #1192 (`Stream review agent events live via JSONL\n  output modes`) addresses is a separate concern: it fixes UX (events visible\n  during the run) but does not change wall-clock duration. This spec is about\n  wall-clock.\n\n## Confirmed cause: workflow asymmetry between the two spawns\n\nThe root cause sits in two files and is verifiable from the code alone, with\nno runtime experiment required.\n\n### Codex spawn\n\n`cmd/entire/cli/agent/codex/reviewer.go` — `buildCodexReviewCmd` composes the\nprompt and then calls `expandCodexBuiltinReview(cfg.Skills)`. That helper\nrewrites the literal string `/review` into 28 words of generic instruction:\n\n> Review the current branch changes and report actionable findings. Prioritize\n> correctness, regressions, security, and missing test coverage. Do not make\n> code changes.\n\nThe rewritten text (plus always-prompt, per-run prompt, scope clause, and\ncheckpoint context) is piped via stdin to `codex exec --json --skip-git-repo-check -`.\n**Codex never sees a slash command. It never invokes its native `/review`.**\n\nJustification in code (`reviewer.go:43-45`): codex's native\n`codex exec review --base <branch>` subcommand rejects an additional prompt,\nso the rewrite was the only way to layer entire's scope/per-run/checkpoint\ncontext on top.\n\nAdditionally, `cmd/entire/cli/agent/codex/discovery.go` —\n`CodexAgent.DiscoverReviewSkills` is a stub that returns `(nil, nil)`. The\ncodex picker therefore offers only curated built-ins (effectively just\n`/review`) + a freeform prompt field. The user cannot configure a richer set\nof codex-side review skills the way they can for claude.\n\n### Claude spawn\n\n`cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/reviewer.go` — `buildReviewCmd` does **no**\npre-processing. The composed prompt is passed verbatim via `claude -p <prompt>`.\nWhen the prompt begins with `/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr\\n/silent-failure-hunter\\n...`,\nclaude's print mode resolves each `/skill-name` into a real skill load.\n\n**Confirmation:** `claude --help` describes the `--bare` flag with the\nexplicit note **\"Skills still resolve via /skill-name\"**, and the\n`--disable-slash-commands` flag is described as **\"Disable all skills\"**. The\nCLI itself documents that `-p` mode resolves slash-commands into skill loads\nby default.\n\n### Net asymmetry\n\n- Claude receives an *executable script*: multiple scripted plugin skills,\n  each with specific steps, file targets, and output expectations.\n- Codex receives an *open-ended instruction* of 28 words: \"review changes,\n  prioritize correctness, regressions, security, missing tests.\"\n\nEven with claude running more skills, the scripted-vs-improvised gap is large\nenough to plausibly account for most of the observed 2-5x. Codex must figure\nout what files to read, what to check for, in what order, with no scaffolding.\nClaude follows the steps each plugin enumerates.\n\n## Remaining hypotheses (residual gap, if any)\n\nThese were *candidates* before the audit. They remain plausible contributors\nto a residual gap but should only be measured **after** the workflow\nasymmetry is fixed, to avoid attributing workflow-driven time to other\ncauses:\n\n1. **`codex exec` non-interactive defaults vs. interactive TUI**: model,\n   reasoning effort, sandbox mode. The TUI may pick faster defaults than\n   `exec`.\n2. **Per-spawn cold-start cost**: MCP servers, codex `hooks.json` firing,\n   seatbelt sandbox warmup, auth token / model cache fetch. Interactive\n   amortizes; every `codex exec` pays.\n3. **Codex `DiscoverReviewSkills` is stubbed** → codex side can never reach\n   plugin-skill parity with claude regardless of duration. Long-term concern,\n   not a wall-clock fix.\n4. **Gemini parallel**: gemini's `gemini -p \" \"` invocation has the same\n   shape questions (slash-command resolution? native review command?\n   paraphrase?) and was deferred from #1192's JSONL switch, so its\n   streaming and duration shape are uncharacterized.\n\n## Fix shapes\n\nFor the confirmed cause, three options. Recommendation: **(A) primary, (B)\nfallback when (A) cannot carry needed context**. (C) is a follow-up project\nreferenced for completeness.\n\n### (A) — Switch to codex's native `/review` via `codex exec review`\n\nUse `codex exec review --json --base <ref>` so codex runs its native review\nworkflow (whatever curated prompt, tool budget, possibly specialized model\nselection that includes).\n\nThe blocker noted in the existing code comment (\"rejects an additional\nprompt\") is real but addressable:\n\n- **Inject context via the codex hook.** `codex/AGENT.md` documents that\n  codex's hook output supports `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` for\n  injecting context into the model. Entire's existing codex hook handler\n  (registered as the `SessionStart` or `UserPromptSubmit` hook) can pick up\n  `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` env vars (the same handshake `entire review` already\n  uses for review-session tagging — see `cmd/entire/cli/review/env.go` and\n  `cmd/entire/cli/lifecycle.go:adoptReviewEnv`) and inject scope / per-run\n  prompt / checkpoint context as `additionalContext` before codex's native\n  review prompt runs.\n- **Hybrid fallback:** if the user has a non-empty per-run prompt, freeform\n  always-prompt, or checkpoint context, and the hook path can't carry it\n  cleanly, fall back to today's paraphrase rather than silently dropping\n  context.\n\n**Tradeoffs:**\n- (+) Codex finally runs its native review workflow.\n- (+) Matches the user's \"fast standalone\" baseline directly.\n- (−) Couples entire to codex's `codex exec review` semantics and the\n  `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` API.\n- (−) Requires changes in `buildCodexReviewCmd` argv shape AND in entire's\n  codex hook handler.\n\n### (B) — Replace the 28-word paraphrase with a much richer scripted prompt\n\nKeep `codex exec -` (stdin prompt), but expand `codexBuiltinReviewPrompt` from\n28 words into a real, scripted review-skill prompt: explicit steps, file\ntargets to inspect, output format. Mirror the structure of a Claude review\nplugin's `SKILL.md` instructions.\n\n**Tradeoffs:**\n- (+) Pure prompt change. No new infrastructure, no hook coupling.\n- (+) Composes with entire's existing context layering.\n- (−) Doesn't capture codex-internal optimizations the native `/review` may\n  have (model selection, tool budget caps, etc.).\n- (−) Maintenance burden: a long prompt embedded as a Go constant.\n\n### (C) — Implement codex's `DiscoverReviewSkills` + ship codex review plugin(s)\n\nFill in the stub at `discovery.go:13` so the picker can offer real codex\nplugin skills. Then a codex review plugin can be authored / installed and\nselected by users.\n\n**Tradeoffs:**\n- (+) Long-term parity with claude's plugin model.\n- (−) Out of scope for a \"fix the speed gap\" PR.\n- (−) Depends on the codex on-disk plugin layout (the stub's comment\n  references `codex-rs/tui/src/slash_command.rs`).\n\n## Experimental protocol — only if residual gap remains after the fix\n\nAfter (A) lands, run a controlled head-to-head to confirm the residual gap\nis small enough to defer further investigation. If material gap remains:\n\n1. **Instrument timing.** Record `time.Now()` per `Event` to a debug log\n   gated behind `ENTIRE_REVIEW_TIMING=1` (no UX impact when unset). The\n   streaming surface from PR #1192 already gives the event boundary.\n2. **Run 3-5 controlled invocations each** of:\n   - `entire review --agent claude-code`\n   - `entire review --agent codex` (post-(A))\n   - `entire review --agent gemini-cli`\n   Same branch, same scope, same per-run prompt.\n3. **Per agent, compute:** time-to-first-event, mean inter-event interval,\n   total wall-clock, total tool-call count, total token counts where\n   available.\n4. **Decision tree:**\n   - High inter-event interval, few tool calls → reasoning effort / model\n     differs. Try `--model` or `reasoning_effort` overrides on `codex exec`.\n   - High tool-call count → codex still exploring more than claude. Refine\n     (A)/(B) prompt or harden hook-injected scope.\n   - High time-to-first-event, normal inter-event → cold-start dominates.\n     Investigate MCP/hooks/seatbelt warmup.\n\n## Gemini parallel\n\nApply the same audit:\n\n- Does `gemini -p` resolve `/skill-name`? `cmd/entire/cli/agent/geminicli/reviewer.go`\n  does no pre-processing, so behavior depends on gemini's print-mode runtime\n  — verify via `gemini --help` for explicit documentation of slash-command\n  behavior (analogous to the `claude --help` finding).\n- Does gemini have a native `/review` that we should be calling? If so, same\n  fix shape as (A): use the native command + inject context via a side\n  channel.\n- Update gemini's `DiscoverReviewSkills` if stubbed (parallel to (C)).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Implementing codex's plugin discovery (fix shape (C)) — separate project.\n- Authoring a codex-side review skill plugin — depends on codex plugin layout\n  verification.\n- Changes to the streaming / parsing infrastructure beyond what PR #1192\n  already lands.\n- Broader review UX changes (multi-agent picker, TUI dashboard, synthesis\n  sink) — independent of the speed gap.\n\n## References\n\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agent/codex/reviewer.go:30-61` — `buildCodexReviewCmd`,\n  `expandCodexBuiltinReview`, `codexBuiltinReviewPrompt`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agent/codex/discovery.go:9-15` — `DiscoverReviewSkills` stub\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agent/codex/AGENT.md:151-165` — codex hook output spec\n  including `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/reviewer.go:28-35` — `buildReviewCmd` (no\n  pre-processing of `/skill-name`)\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agent/geminicli/reviewer.go:30-41` — `buildGeminiReviewCmd`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/review/env.go`, `cmd/entire/cli/lifecycle.go:adoptReviewEnv`\n  — env-var handshake reusable for hook-injected review context\n- PR #1192 (`review-fix-progress-stream`) — JSONL streaming surface that\n  enables event-timing instrumentation\n- `claude --help` — documents `-p` mode skill resolution (`--bare` flag\n  description: \"Skills still resolve via /skill-name\")\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":849,"cache_read_input_tokens":140838,"output_tokens":4487,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"servi