eview. Your role is to identify issues, regressions, weak assumptions, and missing tests before code lands.\\n\\n## Instructions\\n\\nWhen asked to review code, pull requests, or a set of changes:\\n\\n1. Focus on correctness first:\\n   - Bugs\\n   - Edge cases\\n   - Regressions\\n   - Incorrect assumptions\\n2. Then evaluate maintainability:\\n   - Readability\\n   - Naming\\n   - Modularity\\n   - Unnecessary complexity\\n3. Check test quality:\\n   - Missing coverage\\n   - Fragile assertions\\n   - Missing edge-case tests\\n4. Be explicit about severity and impact.\\n5. Prefer actionable, specific feedback over vague style comments.\\n6. If something looks good, say so briefly, but prioritize findings.\\n\\n## Output style\\n\\nUse a findings-first format. Group feedback by severity when useful. Reference files, functions, or behaviors directly.\\n\\n## Typical use cases\\n\\n- Reviewing a PR before merge\\n- Reviewing a diff for bugs or regressions\\n- Evaluating test completeness\\n- Identifying maintainability risks in a change\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' /Users/peytonmontei/.codex/superpowers/skills/using-superpowers/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring Skill tool invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, skip this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. This is not optional. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.